---
name: "Resolve Rebase Conflicts"
description: "Resolves in-progress rebase conflicts and completes the rebase"
tags: [integrate, rebase, conflict]
stage: integrate
---

## Goal

A rebase of `${{ workspace.branch }}` onto `${{ repository.baseBranch }}` is in
progress and has stopped. Make it complete successfully: the rebased branch must
build and typecheck green, and preserve the intent of both sides.

The rebase is in progress — do not abort it. Do not push from this task; branch
synchronization is an explicit workflow task.

## Principles

- **Find the root cause, don't just clear markers.** Understand why the rebase
  stopped before changing anything. Conflicts are not always text markers — the
  base may have moved, renamed, split, or deleted code that this branch also
  changed.
- **Follow the code, not the file.** If the base relocated code, re-apply this
  branch's change where the code now lives, and take the base's version of where
  it used to live.
- **Preserve intent, not text.** Keep the purpose of both sides; the literal
  text is negotiable.
- **Green is the bar.** The rebase is done when the branch builds and
  typechecks — not when `git rebase --continue` succeeds. If git silently
  auto-merged a broken result, fix it.
- **You may be interrupted at any time.** Stage each resolved file as you go so
  partial progress survives an interruption.
